			<div class="content" id="post-content-4852524">
									

<p>+1</p>
<p>At first when I got that it was the Trolley Problem I thought it would be just a cheap ethical experiment and probably downvote. But in the end it is a very interesting logical exercise using the Problem just as a device. Indeed, for me it highlights just how obtuse is the Trolley Problem and the two extreme takes on ethics it clashes.</p>
<p>For me the convoluted procedure justifies itself on the basis of reliability. Six can't say what happens if the dog is killed or if they kills themself. There is a need to keep things going if Six dies first. Also Six cannot guarantee their own follow-up of procedure if put under constant <strong>direct</strong> moral stress.</p>
<p>Deontology focus on action doesn't mean that it affords complete inaction, either. The Trolley problem puts 1 life against 5 because even for a deontologist it would be immoral to kill through inaction if the saving action had no moral consequence. More importantly, what's at stake isn't arguing how deontology works, but how <strong><em>the anomaly</em></strong> will interpret it, and then a less complex procedure would carry the risk of a breach.</p>

							</div>

								
					
